Thanks to Ohle C for pointing out that a tin of green dragon and
a unihorn work better than my suggestion of tinned giants.  I
was trying to come up with reasons for tinning and I remember
tinning giants.  Here's a better reason for tinning giants in the
Valkyrie quest - Giant corpses have far more nutrition than
giant tins.  Eat the corpses and you end up spending a lot of
time waiting between meals; tin them and more can be eaten
to concentrate the benefits.

> Why would you need tins of nymph? Tengus can also give you teleportitis.
> The chance for getting teleportitis from a tengu is actually higher than
> the chance for getting teleport control. (I think that is the reason why
> it is a good idea to tin many of them and eat all the tins at once
> instead of eating each of them fresh).

First reason: There are more nymphs plus tengu in the
dungeon that tengu alone.  Having more options is good.

> In my last game I ate a tengu (fresh, not tinned) in the hope of getting
> teleport control. It gave me teleportitis instead. But I was lucky and
> had a ring of teleport control. I only got intrinsic teleport control
> when my wizard reached level 17.
>
> If I remember correctly, one nymph or leprechaun is enough for getting
> teleportitis.

Second reason: If a nymph or leprechaun tin is a sure thing to
gain the first intrinsic then you have more chances with tengu
to gain the other intrinsic.  I think one reason tengu is uncertain
is it can give either intrinsic - Having teleportitis merely converts
a meal that would have given it to a meal that only gives
nutrition.  But if you would have gotten teleport control you get
teleportitis instead I think so there is some benefit to being
certain you can have teleportitus before starting to eat tengu.



